# Findings & Business Recommendations

## Key Findings

### Finding 1 — Graph structure is the critical signal
Isolation Forest on 167 raw anonymized features achieved 0% recall.
It caught zero fraud. Not because the algorithm is weak, but because
anonymized features alone carry no structural information about how
transactions relate to each other in the network.

Adding a single graph feature — transaction degree — and switching to
a supervised, class-balanced Random Forest produced:
- 99% accuracy
- 100% precision on illicit transactions
- 88% recall
- AUC 0.997

The feature matters more than the algorithm.

### Finding 2 — Class imbalance must be addressed explicitly
The dataset contains 42,019 licit transactions and 4,545 illicit ones — a 9:1 ratio.
A naive model predicting everything as licit achieves 90% accuracy while catching
zero fraud. Class-weighted training forces the model to treat each illicit transaction
as 9 times more important during learning.

### Finding 3 — Precision matters more than recall in this domain
Logistic Regression achieved 93% recall but only 46% precision.
More than half its fraud flags were false alarms.
In an investigative context where analyst time is the bottleneck,
a 46% precision rate means investigators spend more time chasing
dead leads than confirmed fraud. The system loses credibility fast.

Random Forest achieved 99.75% precision — nearly every flag is real.

---

## Business Recommendations

**1. Deploy as a pre-screening layer**
Out of 9,313 transactions in the test set, the model flagged 799 as illicit.
Of those 799 flags, 797 were confirmed fraud — a false alarm rate of 0.25%.
Without this model, an investigator reviewing all 9,313 transactions manually
would spend 93% of their time on legitimate activity. This model reduces
that wasted effort to near zero.

**2. Threshold tuning based on operational capacity**
The model assigns a fraud probability score to every transaction, not just
a binary flag. The current threshold is 50%. Lowering it catches more fraud
but generates more false alarms — suitable when analyst capacity is high.
Raising it means every flag is near-certain fraud — suitable when
investigator time is scarce. This is a business decision, not a technical one.

**3. Retrain every 6 months minimum**
The dataset covers 49 time steps of real Bitcoin activity. Fraud patterns
in later time steps differ from earlier ones. Mixing techniques evolve,
new scam structures emerge. A model trained on historical data degrades
against new patterns. Retraining on fresh labeled data quarterly
maintains detection performance.

**4. Graph features are non-negotiable**
Transaction degree alone transformed model performance from 0% to 88% recall.
Future iterations should incorporate additional graph metrics:
- Clustering coefficient — how tightly connected a wallet's neighbors are
- Betweenness centrality — whether a wallet sits on many shortest paths, typical of mixing hubs
- PageRank — recursive importance based on connection quality, not just quantity

**5. Address label latency for production deployment**
The Elliptic dataset provides clean labels assigned after the fact by forensic analysts.
In production, labels arrive weeks or months after transactions — if at all.
Law enforcement confirmations are slow. Exchange reports are incomplete.
Production deployment requires an online learning architecture that
incorporates delayed feedback without full retraining. This is the
hardest open problem in production fraud detection.
